## 8. EXPECTED RESULTS & PERFORMANCE PREDICTIONS

Phase 1 is expected to achieve a separation efficiency of 20–25 % with ice purity >60 %. The measured acoustic attenuation will determine the viable frequency. Triboelectric charge is predicted to be 100–1000 nC total, confirming the non‑thermal charging model. The specific energy will be approximately 16 kWh/kg, which is not yet competitive with advanced thermal systems but demonstrates the feasibility of hybrid acoustic‑electrostatic separation.

A roadmap to commercial viability is defined: Phase 2 will introduce optimised timing, higher electric fields, and pulsed‑operation transducers, targeting 40 % efficiency and 5 kWh/kg. Phase 3 will integrate a resonant acoustic cavity (Q ~100), reducing power input a hundredfold and bringing the specific energy below 0.1 kWh/kg, enabling truly economic lunar water extraction.

