## APPENDIX B: DERIVATION OF TRIBELECTRIC CHARGE ACCUMULATION IN A VIBRATED GRANULAR BED

We model the charging of an ice particle during repeated collisions with regolith grains in a fluidised bed. Each contact is treated as a parallel-plate capacitor discharging a small charge determined by the work function difference.

Let $A_c$ be the effective contact area, typically $\sim 10^{-11}$ m² for micron-scale asperities. The contact gap at maximum stress is $d_c \approx 0.3$ nm (atomic spacing). The work function difference between water ice and silicate minerals is $\Delta\phi \approx 1$–$2$ V (Forward et al. 2009). The charge transferred per contact is

$$
q_1 = \epsilon_0 A_c \frac{\Delta\phi}{d_c},
$$

where $\epsilon_0 = 8.85\times10^{-12}$ F m⁻¹. Using conservative values $A_c = 5\times10^{-12}$ m², $\Delta\phi = 1$ V, $d_c = 3\times10^{-10}$ m gives

$$
q_1 \approx 8.85\times10^{-12} \cdot 5\times10^{-12} / 3\times10^{-10} \approx 1.5\times10^{-13} \text{ C}.
$$

Real contacts may involve multiple asperities; experimental data (Forward et al.) indicate effective single-collision charges on the order of $10^{-15}$–$10^{-14}$ C per micron-sized particle.

During a vibration period of 20 s at 300 Hz, a particle undergoes many collisions. In a dense fluidised bed with particle volume fraction $\phi_s \sim 0.3$, the collision frequency can be estimated as

$$
f_{\text{coll}} \approx n_p \sigma v_{\text{rel}},
$$

with $n_p$ the number density of particles, $\sigma \approx \pi (a_p + a_g)^2$ the collision cross-section, and $v_{\text{rel}}$ the relative velocity. For 50 µm ice particles in a bed of similar-sized regolith grains, $f_{\text{coll}} \sim 10^4$–$10^5$ s⁻¹, giving $N_{\text{coll}} \sim 2\times10^5$–$2\times10^6$ over 20 s.

Charge accumulation saturates when the repulsive electric field from the particle’s own charge prevents further electron transfer; the saturation charge is roughly

$$
q_{\text{sat}} \approx \epsilon_0 A_c E_{\text{breakdown}},
$$

where $E_{\text{breakdown}}$ is the field at which air (or vacuum) breakdown occurs around the contact, typically $\sim 3\times10^6$ V m⁻¹ for small gaps. This yields $q_{\text{sat}} \sim 10^{-13}$–$10^{-12}$ C, i.e., 0.1–1 pC. Forward et al. measured net charges on 100 µm simulant grains of 1–10 pC after prolonged fluidisation. Thus, a 50 µm ice particle might carry $\sim 0.5$–$5$ pC.

Multiplying by the number of ice particles ($\sim 10^8$ for 8.6 g of 50 µm grains) gives a total collectable charge of $5\times10^{-5}$ to $5\times10^{-4}$ C (50–500 µC). The Phase 1 test will measure the integrated current, providing the first experimental validation of these estimates at 100 K.

