## APPENDIX D: ENERGY BUDGET CALCULATION DETAILS

**Vibration power.**  
The shaker armature moves sinusoidally: $x(t) = A \sin(2\pi f t)$, with $A=2$ mm, $f=300$ Hz. The velocity is $v(t) = 2\pi f A \cos(2\pi f t)$, rms velocity $v_{\text{rms}} = 2\pi f A / \sqrt{2}$. The force required to accelerate the regolith bed plus container is approximately $F = m_{\text{load}} a$, where $m_{\text{load}} \approx 5$ kg (container + regolith + cold plate) and $a = (2\pi f)^2 A \approx 7.1$ m s⁻² (0.72 g). Thus $F \approx 35.5$ N. Mechanical power:

$$
P_{\text{vib}} = F \, v_{\text{rms}} = 35.5 \times \frac{2\pi \times 300 \times 0.002}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 35.5 \times 2.67 \approx 95 \text{ W}.
$$

With amplifier and motor losses (~40%), total electrical power $\approx 133$ W. Over 10 s: 1.33 kJ.

**Acoustic power.**  
64 PZT-8 elements, each $4\times4\times1$ mm. At 100 K the impedance magnitude $|Z| \approx 500$ Ω. Driven with $200$ V peak sinusoidal waveform, the average power per element is $P_1 = V_{\text{rms}}^2 / R = (141)^2 / 500 \approx 40$ W. Total continuous power would be $64 \times 40 = 2.56$ kW. To limit heat load, a 50 % duty cycle is used (10 ms on, 10 ms off), so average acoustic electrical power is $1.28$ kW. Over 20 s: $25.6$ kJ.

**Electrostatic power.**  
High-voltage supply output: 2 kV, average current ~1 mA (mainly leakage and charging currents). $P_{\text{es}} = 2000 \times 0.001 = 2$ W. Over 20 s: 0.04 kJ.

**Cryocooler.**  
Continuous operation at 10 W electrical input to maintain 100 K setpoint (accounting for all heat loads). Over 60 s cycle: 0.6 kJ.

**Total per batch = 1.33 + 25.6 + 0.04 + 0.6 = 27.57 kJ.**  
Ice extracted per batch (20 % efficiency) = 1.72 g. Specific energy $= 27.57/0.00172 \approx 16\,030$ kJ/kg $= 4.45$ kWh/kg. (Minor update from earlier draft due to refined vibration force; still within 4–16 kWh/kg range.)

